# {{name}}

A widget package for the [Pebbles](https://github.com/pebbles-hq/pebbles) GUI
framework.

```rust
use pebbles::prelude::*;
use {{name_snake}}::{callout, CalloutKind};

fn screen() -> impl IntoWidget {
    column(children![
        callout("Heads up", "This widget ships as its own crate."),
        callout("Careful", "Something needs a look.").kind(CalloutKind::Warning),
    ])
}
```

See it run:

```sh
cargo run --example demo
cargo test
```

## Conventions this package follows

Worth keeping as you extend it — they are what make a widget package feel
built-in rather than bolted on:

1. **Lowercase constructor functions.** `callout(..)` builds the widget;
   `Callout` is only a type name. Never expose `Type::new()` as the documented
   way to build something.
2. **Public API only.** Depend on `pebbles-widgets` (catalog + theme),
   `pebbles-core` (the widget/reactive contract) and
   `pebbles-render`/`pebbles-foundation` (primitives). Never reach into
   framework internals — if you need to, that is a framework gap to report.
3. **Theme, don't hardcode.** Read `theme().colors` so the widget follows
   light/dark automatically.
4. **Test with `pebbles-testing`.** `Harness` owns mount/frame/draw/input, so a
   framework pipeline change does not touch your tests.
5. **Ship an example.** `examples/demo.rs` is how people evaluate the widget.

For a fully worked reference package, see
[`pebbles-markdown`](https://github.com/pebbles-hq/pebbles-markdown).

